raw:
{
"slots": {
"question": [
{
"claim": "The paper investigates whether Democratic Party advertisements labeling Donald Trump as a 'convicted criminal' negatively affect Democrats' perceptions of individuals with criminal records and their feelings of political representation, and whether a counter-narrative featuring a formerly incarcerated legislator can mitigate these effects.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "To test for the possibility of stigmatizing language spillover, this preliminary study employs a survey experiment testing exposure to the Party’s advertisement, labeling Trump as a “convicted criminal” (n = 814) relative to no advertisement (n = 813), on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with records and several dimensions of perceived representation.",
"page": 1
},
{
"text": "Thus, this study explores what may have been the impact of propagating such labels on Democrats’ perceptions of people with criminal records, and to what extent does the propagation of such stigmatizing terminology impact how well Democrats perceive to be represented by their own elected officials?",
"page": 4
}
]
}
],
"central_claim": [
{
"claim": "Exposure to Democratic advertisements labeling Trump as a 'convicted criminal' significantly worsens Democrats' perceptions of individuals with criminal records but improves their perceived feelings of representation, while a counter-narrative featuring a formerly incarcerated representative largely ameliorates the negative perceptions of individuals with records.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Results suggest that exposure to the Trump advertisement with the “convicted criminal” label had significant, negative impacts on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with records, but improved perceived feelings of representation.",
"page": 1
},
{
"text": "Findings suggest that the counter-stereotype vastly ameliorated the negative effects of the advertisement on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with criminal records.",
"page": 1
}
]
}
],
"evidence": [
{
"claim": "The study uses a survey experiment with 2,439 self-identified Democrats recruited from Prolific, randomized into three groups: a control group, a group exposed only to a Trump advertisement labeled 'convicted criminal,' and a group exposed to a counter-narrative about a formerly incarcerated state representative before seeing the advertisement.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Thus, the 2,439 participants were allocated approximately evenly across one of three different treatment groups: a) the Control group (n = 813); b) the Trump Ad Alone group (n = 814); and c) the Counter-Narrative First group (n = 812).",
"page": 4
},
{
"text": "The survey was fielded through Prolific, an online survey platform... 2,500 participants were recruited for this study, between July 12th and 13th of 2025... The latter inclusion criteria is important, given the purpose of the study is to understand the impacts of Democratic Party advertising on feelings of being adequately represented... thus the study sample focused only on their constituents.",
"page": 8
}
]
}
],
"key_findings": [
{
"claim": "Exposure to the Trump advertisement alone significantly decreased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records compared to the control group.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Hypothesis Set 2 (H2) has only two hypotheses... Both H2A and H2B were supported, namely, exposure to the Trump advertisement alone significantly decreased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records ( β̂0 = −0.835, SE = 0.044)",
"page": 11
}
]
},
{
"claim": "Presenting a counter-narrative featuring a formerly incarcerated legislator before the Trump advertisement significantly increased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records by more than 0.8 standard deviations compared to seeing the advertisement alone.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "The most profound impact of the counter-narrative, unsurprisingly, given the content of the counter-narrative, was on the perceptions of individuals with criminal records (H1B): exposure to the counter-narrative prior to being presented with the Trump advertisement increased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records by more than 0.8 standard deviation (SD) units, compared to those presented with the Trump advertisement alone ( β̂2 = 0.83, SE = 0.041).",
"page": 11
}
]
},
{
"claim": "Exposure to the Trump advertisement alone significantly increased Democrats' perceived feelings of representation and their belief that elected officials are motivated by public service rather than self-interest.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "exposure to the Trump advertisement alone significantly decreased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records ( β̂0 = −0.835, SE = 0.044) and significantly increased participants beliefs that elected officials’ motivations were to serve the public, versus serving their own self-interest ( β̂0 = 0.130, SE = 0.049).",
"page": 11
}
]
},
{
"claim": "The counter-narrative significantly decreased support for the Trump advertisement and worsened perceptions of the quality of Democratic representation in federal government compared to the advertisement alone.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Starting with H1A, exposure to the counter-narrative significantly decreased support for the Trump advertisement, relative to the Trump advertisement alone ( β̂2 = −0.172 SE = 0.048)... Additionally, evidence supported H1D, namely that exposure to the counter-narrative significantly decreased quality of Democratic representation in federal government, relative to just Trump advertisement exposure ( β̂2 = −0.332, SE = 0.048).",
"page": 11
}
]
},
{
"claim": "Participants who identified as system-impacted reported more positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records overall, and the negative effects of the Trump advertisement on these perceptions were significantly more damaging for this group.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "those that identified as being system-impacted held more positive perceptions of individuals with records, relative to those who did not disclose system-impact status, on average... the Trump advertisement exposure appeared to be significantly more damaging to perceptions of individuals with criminal records for those who are system-impacted.",
"page": 13
}
]
}
],
"scope_and_limits": [
{
"claim": "The study does not show the durability of effects over time, how effects vary by repeated exposure, or the translation of these attitude shifts into actual voting behavior, due to its single time-point design.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Specifically, the single time-point survey experimental approach is a useful first step, but leaves unanswered several key questions related to the ability of these results to replicate: a) for how long do the effects of the counter-narrative prime before the advertisement and the advertisement itself persist; b) how may effects vary by single versus repeated exposure to the advertisement and/or the counter-narrative prime?; and b) to what extent would these treatments translate to shifted decision-making and behavior, as opposed to self-reported perceptions and attitudes?",
"page": 17
}
]
},
{
"claim": "The study's design constraints, specifically the lack of a full factorial design, prevent isolating the impact of the counter-narrative alone without the advertisement, and the use of a single image prevents disentangling the impact of the visual from the text.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "First, the ideal version of this survey experiment would have been to utilize a full factorial design (i.e., including the exposure to the counter-narrative perspective arm alone)... failing to include the counter-narrative only arm limits the ability to interpret the additional impact of including it with the advertisements... Additionally, as the advertisement... was presented consistently across treatment arms, it is not possible to disentangle the impact of the image from the stigmatizing terminology imposed",
"page": 17
}
]
},
{
"claim": "The study does not show whether these results would replicate during an active campaign period or in less-transparent designs, as the survey was fielded after the election outcome was known.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Importantly, when such studies are implemented will be important, as this current study was conducted in July of 2025, after the outcome of the election was known and not actively during the campaign. Whether these results replicate during campaign periods is an open question",
"page": 17
}
]
}
],
"theoretical_positioning": [
{
"claim": "The authors place the paper in the tradition of social categorization theory to explain the spillover effects of labeling Trump, and they extend this framework by testing a 'counter-stereotype' intervention to mitigate the negative spillover.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Leaning upon social categorization theory, propagating such labels on Trump may thereby spillover, impacting constituents’ perspectives of individuals with records more broadly.",
"page": 1
},
{
"text": "Specifically, social categorization theory articulates the implications and process of how individuals come to assign and distinguish individuals into groups, with much of the theory and evidence pointing to the importance of category salience and the role of group exemplars and prototypes [16].",
"page": 2
}
]
}
],
"interlocutor_claim": [
{
"claim": "The paper disputes the claim that negative campaign advertisements are empirically supported to aid election success, citing meta-analytic evidence that suggests they do not.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Additionally, meta-analytic evidence does not empirically support the use of negative campaign advertisements to aid election success [7].",
"page": 2
}
]
},
{
"claim": "The paper builds on the claim that experimental evidence shows policy positions and elected officials taking action have a stronger causal effect on feelings of being represented than affect or emotion.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "In fact, experimental evidence suggests that policy positions and elected officials taking action tend to have a stronger causal effect on feelings of being represented, rather than affect [37].",
"page": 3
}
]
}
]
}
}
